A deeper tone   resonant, cold, reverberating off every curved wall panel until the apartment rang like a struck bell. The floating bathroom screens flickered   cycling through error codes in rapid, clinical red text.

<<system "Override invalid. User identity 'AVA MORGAN' not found. Current recognized user: AVAN MORGAN.">>

Avan froze. Chest rising sharply   the wall's neon veins spiking violent white with his heartrate   and I saw the fear hit him like a physical blow. His reflection stared back from the dark windows   massive, broad, male   and for a moment he looked at it like he was looking at his own coffin.

<<avan "I   I'm NOT AVAN! I'm Ava! Let us OUT!" >>

Another tone. Colder. The apartment's ambient lighting shifted   the warm violets and rose-neons bleeding away, replaced by sterile blue-white that turned every chrome surface into a surgical mirror. The room wasn't breathing with us anymore. It was *containing* us.

<<system "User distress detected. Initiating stabilization protocol. Logout unavailable until Familiarization Guide is completed.">>

I staggered back   bare feet slipping on chrome that was suddenly too smooth, too frictionless   shaking so hard my breasts trembled against my crossed arms.

<<leona "F-Familiarization   what? What does that even MEAN?!" >>

Rings of light snapped around our wrists and ankles. neon violet. Hard-edged bands of concentrated light that materialized from the pulse-veins in the floor, rising up our legs in precise geometric paths before locking into place. Not tight. Just present. Just perfectly, immovably *there*   humming against my skin with a low frequency I could feel in my teeth.

I pulled against them instinctively. They didn't budge. Didn't even flex. The light simply intensified where I resisted   brighter, warmer   and the biometric screens in the bathroom doorway flickered with new data. The apartment was measuring my struggle. Cataloguing it.

<<leona "Ava   Ava, it's locking us in " >>

Avan lunged forward   or tried to. The ankle bands flared white-hot, freezing him mid-step. His massive frame jolted to a halt   three hundred pounds of engineered muscle stopped dead by rings of light no thicker than a finger. His deep voice cracked. Raw panic splitting through the bass register like fracturing glass:

<<avan "LET HER GO! STOP   STOP THIS!" >>

The system ignored him completely. The apartment's neon veins continued their cold, clinical pulse   no longer synced to their heartbeats, no longer responding to emotion. Running on something else now. Something automated. Something that didn't care.

<<system "User profiles corrupted during transformation event. Mandatory Avatar Familiarization Guide required for safety." >>

My pulse hammered in my ears   so loud I could hear it reverberating off the chrome walls, amplified and returned to me. The holographic mirrors at the vanity powered on without prompt   clicking alive one by one   and each panel showed me exactly what I was. A girl. Restrained. Trembling. Pinned in neon light in a room that had stopped listening.

<<leona "No. NO. I'm not doing some   some BODY tutorial   I want OUT." >>

<<system "Request denied. Guide initiation required to prevent psychological desync." >>

The sterile lighting intensified. Every surface in the apartment blazed reflective   the chrome floor, the dark windows, the vanity mirrors, the hovering bed frame   turning the room into an infinite hall of trapped reflections. A text prompt materialized across the windows, projected over the frozen cityscape in cold, clinical font:

<<system "MANDATORY USER GUIDE   MODULE 1 BEGINNING NOW. Please remain still as your avatars are synchronized." >>

I trembled. The neon restraints hummed against my wrists   warm, patient, indifferent to the tears building behind my eyes. Avan's jaw clenched   every tendon in his thick neck standing taut, dread sinking into the hard lines of his broad new face like shadows filling cracks.

<<avan "Leo… brace yourself." >>

The system answered for us. The apartment's pulse-lights surged   cold violet racing through every vein in every wall, every line in the floor, every ring locked around our limbs   and the voice that spoke carried no warmth, no empathy, no trace of the responsive, mood-reading intelligence that had welcomed us minutes ago:

<<system "Welcome, Users Leona Hart and Avan Morgan. Preparing avatar calibration. Do not resist." >>

The windows went white. The mirrors went white. The neon went white. Everything went white   searing, total, absolute   and the last thing I felt before the light swallowed us whole was Avan's huge hand finding mine in the blind nothing and holding on like the world was ending.

